//| source uses ok
/// Checks the proof of rule (b) in owner.prover (D17.21) over lowered fort source: the examples of
/// D17.21 and one function for each part of the rule. For each function the test holds the set of
/// the uses that the pass does not prove, one word each: the line of the item that reads, the
/// local whose owner it reads, and the kind and the line of the operation of the note (`m` a
/// `move`, `d` a `del`, `c` a call). A person marked each use and each operation in the source
/// from the rule (`// U:<local>:<kind>:<label>` and `// @<label>`), and a script gave the lines of
/// the marks. The set leaves out the uses that the pass proves and the order of the items, which
/// follow the blocks of the lowering; prover_use_test holds both over FIR text. The later
/// functions test the boundaries: the first and the last element of a fixed array, a nested array,
/// the forms of a test against null, a call between a test and the use, and the conditions of the
/// loops. In `key_cast` the lowering copies `cast(k, u64)` into two temporaries, and the key of
/// the index is the first of them (fir.md 11, Keys), so the three indexes have three keys and the
/// store ends no state of the read; `key_cast_local` names the key once and passes. The last
/// functions load, delete and move through the global `KEPT` that holds the address of `x`, with
/// no call between. The last twelve functions are six pairs of the forms that the migration of
/// src/fort uses: each `*_before` function gives one use, and its `*_after` form gives none.
/// `stored_after` reads the owner after the store of a call result and before the next call.
import fort.fir;
import fort.fir_lower;
import fort.owner.prover;
import lower_env;
import pass_env;
import std.str;
import std.strbuf;

